`LocalNotificationModifiedExpensePushParams` is defined as `LocalNotificationModifiedExpenseParams & { ... }`, and this PR also adds `formatPhoneNumber` to the base `LocalNotificationModifiedExpenseParams`. Re-declaring `formatPhoneNumber` again in the intersection is redundant — the property is already inherited from the base type, so this line adds no new information and increases maintenance overhead.

Remove the redundant re-declaration from `LocalNotificationModifiedExpensePushParams`:

```ts
type LocalNotificationModifiedExpensePushParams = LocalNotificationModifiedExpenseParams & {
    usesIcon?: boolean;
    policyTags: OnyxEntry<PolicyTagLists>;
    policy?: OnyxEntry<Policy>;
};
```

Removing `isRulesRevampEnabled` collapsed the header title to the hardcoded `'workspace.rules.spendRules.restrictCardSpendTitle'` key, so `titleKey` is no longer read anywhere in the component. But `titleKey: TranslationPaths;` is still declared as a required prop on `SpendRulePageBaseProps`, and callers (`AddSpendRulePage`, `EditSpendRulePage`) still pass it, leaving a dead required prop.

Drop `titleKey` from `SpendRulePageBaseProps` and remove it from each call site:

```tsx
type SpendRulePageBaseProps = {
    policyID: string;
    ruleID?: string;
    testID: string;

    /** Where the Control upgrade page should return to. Defaults to the workspace Rules page. */
    upgradeBackTo?: Route;
};
```

Removing `isRulesRevampEnabled` collapsed the header title to the hardcoded `'workspace.rules.merchantRules.expenseDefaultsTitle'` key, so `titleKey` is no longer read anywhere in the component. But `titleKey: TranslationPaths;` is still declared as a required prop on `MerchantRulePageBaseProps`, and every caller (`AddMerchantRulePage`, `EditMerchantRulePage`, etc.) still passes it. This leaves a dead, required prop threaded through the whole component tree.

Drop `titleKey` from `MerchantRulePageBaseProps` and remove it from each call site:

```tsx
type MerchantRulePageBaseProps = {
    policyID: string;
    ruleID?: string;
    /** Pre-scopes the category default when creating a rule (e.g. from the category details RHP). */
    initialCategoryName?: string;
    testID: string;
};
```

This PR removes the `isOnSearch` argument from `setupMergeTransactionDataAndNavigate` and from the `MERGE_TRANSACTION_*` `getRoute` builders, so nothing ever sets `isOnSearch` on the route params anymore. Yet `isOnSearch?: boolean;` is still declared on all four `MergeTransactionNavigatorParamList` screens (`DYNAMIC_LIST_PAGE`, `DYNAMIC_RECEIPT_PAGE`, `DYNAMIC_DETAILS_PAGE`, `DYNAMIC_CONFIRMATION_PAGE`). The `DynamicMergeTransactionsListPage`, `DynamicReceiptReviewPage`, and `DynamicDetailsReviewPage` no longer read it at all (destructuring was removed in this PR), and `DynamicConfirmationPage` still reads it but it will now always be `undefined`, silently falling back to `isSearchTopmostFullScreenRoute()`. This leaves a dead, unsettable param on the interface — the same cleanup that dropped `backTo` should drop `isOnSearch`.

Remove the now-unused `isOnSearch?: boolean;` member from each of the four screen param types:

```ts
type MergeTransactionNavigatorParamList = {
    [SCREENS.MERGE_TRANSACTION.DYNAMIC_LIST_PAGE]: {
        transactionID: string;
    };
    [SCREENS.MERGE_TRANSACTION.DYNAMIC_RECEIPT_PAGE]: {
        transactionID: string;
    };
    [SCREENS.MERGE_TRANSACTION.DYNAMIC_DETAILS_PAGE]: {
        transactionID: string;
    };
    [SCREENS.MERGE_TRANSACTION.DYNAMIC_CONFIRMATION_PAGE]: {
        transactionID: string;
    };
};
```

If `DynamicConfirmationPage` still needs to know whether it is on Search, rely solely on `isSearchTopmostFullScreenRoute()` there and drop the now-always-undefined `isOnSearch` read.

This PR removes the table row loading/skeleton mechanism: `isLoading` and `skeletonReasonAttributes` were dropped, and the `{!!isLoading && LoadingComponent ? (...) : (...)}` block that was the only consumer of `LoadingComponent` was deleted. As a result `LoadingComponent` is now declared and destructured but never referenced in the component body, and its only caller (`WorkspaceCompanyCardsTableRow`) stopped passing it in this same PR. An unused prop adds surface area and is misleading about the component's capabilities.

Remove the now-dead `LoadingComponent` prop from both the `TableRowProps` type and the destructured parameter list:

```tsx
type TableRowProps = Omit<PressableWithFeedbackProps, 'accessible'> & {
    // ...
    /** Whether or not the row should animate in highlighted */
    shouldAnimateInHighlight?: boolean;

    // (remove the LoadingComponent prop here)

    /** Attributes for when the client is offline and there is an error related to the table row */
    offlineWithFeedback?: OfflineWithFeedbackProps;
    // ...
};

export default function TableRow({
    // ...
    shouldAnimateInHighlight,
    // (remove LoadingComponent from the destructure)
    onPress,
    // ...
}: TableRowProps) {
```

`SearchAdvancedFiltersContentProps` extends `SearchFilterCommonProps`, which includes `selectionListTextInputStyle`, `selectionListStyle`, `autoFocus`, `ready`, and `footer`. However, the component function at line 35 only destructures `{filterKey, values, policyIDQuery, onChange, components}` and never references any of the `SearchFilterCommonProps` fields. These props are accepted by the type but completely unused in the implementation.

Remove the `SearchFilterCommonProps` intersection from the type definition:
```tsx
type SearchAdvancedFiltersContentProps = {
    filterKey: SearchFilter['key'];
    values: Partial<SearchAdvancedFiltersForm> | undefined;
    policyIDQuery: string[] | undefined;
    components: { ... };
    onChange: (values: Partial<SearchAdvancedFiltersForm>) => void;
};
```
Also remove the unused `autoFocus` and `ready` props from the call sites in `SearchAdvancedFiltersContentBase.tsx`.

`HasSelectorProps` extends `SearchFilterSelectionListProps` which includes `selectionListTextInputStyle`, and the call site in `index.tsx` passes this prop. However, `HasSelector` does not destructure or forward `selectionListTextInputStyle` to `MultiSelect`. Every other selector component in this directory (e.g., `CardSelector`, `CategorySelector`, `InSelector`, `CurrencySelector`) forwards this prop to its inner component. This appears to be an accidental omission that would cause the text input styling to be lost.

Add `selectionListTextInputStyle` to the destructured props and forward it to `MultiSelect`:
```tsx
function HasSelector({value = [], selectionListTextInputStyle, selectionListStyle, footer, onChange}: HasSelectorProps) {
    // ...
    return (
        <MultiSelect
            items={items}
            value={multiSelectValues}
            selectionListTextInputStyle={selectionListTextInputStyle}
            // ... rest of props
        />
    );
}
```

The `betas` parameter is added back as optional in `PrepareOnboardingOnyxDataParams` "for backwards compatibility," but after this PR it is never used inside `prepareOnboardingOnyxData` -- the destructuring of `betas` from the params object is removed, and every reference to it in the function body (the `Permissions.isBetaEnabled` call and the `isPhase1Cohort` logic) is also removed. Keeping an unused parameter in the type definition adds unnecessary complexity.

Since callers already pass `betas` (e.g., `completeOnboarding`, `buildPolicyData`, `TrackExpense`), the cleanest fix is to remove `betas` from `PrepareOnboardingOnyxDataParams` entirely and stop passing it at every call site in this same PR. TypeScript will flag every caller that still passes it, making the cleanup straightforward:

```ts
type PrepareOnboardingOnyxDataParams = {
    // ... other fields ...
    isSelfTourViewed?: boolean;
    // Remove betas entirely -- no longer consumed
    hasCompletedGuidedSetupFlow?: boolean;
};
```

